252,282 roughly. For 2.41: 414.94 vs 414.462... hmm, dual: maybe the volume is per two strokes? 2×2.41=4.82 → 1000/4.82/... no. 1000/4.82 = 207.5. Hmm no. Actually for FMR-Dual, maybe 414.462 = 2 × 207.231, whatever. Anyway, \"414,462\" is a decimal number 414.462 with comma as decimal separator (per rule: convert commas). So revolutions = 414.462 per m³? And [m^-3] column value \"1\"? That seems like \"per m³\"? Actually \"revolutions: 414,462\" and \"[m^-3]: 1\" might mean 414.462 revolutions per m³ — the \"1\" might be part of the fraction m^-3 display like \"1/m³\"? Hmm, could be \"[m⁻³]\" unit column and the \"1\" is... hmm.\n\nActually maybe the transfer relationship is expressed as a fraction: 414,462 / 1 m⁻³? Like a gear ratio written as numerator/denominator? I think the two columns are \"revolutions\" (numerator) and \"[m^-3]\" (denominator), meaning 414.462 revolutions per m³... wait m^-3 means per m³? m^-3 = 1/m³. So \"414.462 revolutions, 1 per m³\"? That would be 414.462 revolutions and unit... \n\nHmm, alternatively: transfer relationship = revolutions per m³ where the value is 414.462 (with the unit 1/m³, and \"1\" is odd). Let me just preserve: column \"basic_transfer_relationship_revolutions\" value 414.462 (decimal fixed) and column \"basic_transfer_relationship_per_m3\" value 1.\n\nWait, actually, maybe \"414,462\" is actually two integers: 414 and 462 (a list of gear tooth counts)? Like \"414,462\" meaning gear combinations 414 or 462? Hmm. But given G values and other cells, \"80 or 100\" is explicit \"or\". \"414,462\" could be decimal. Given the per-m³ calc matches 157.676 ≈ 1000/6.34, decimal comma is right for that. 1000/6.34 = 157.728... let me compute: 6.34 × 157.676 = 999.87. Close. 1000/157.676 = 6.342. Yes matches V=6.34. For 3.96: 252.282 → 1000/252.282 = 3.9638 ≈ 3.96 ✓. For 2.41: 414.462 → 1000/414.462 = 2.4128 ≈ 2.41 ✓. So yes, decimal commas. revolutions per m³ = e.g. 414.462, and the \"1\" in [m^-3] column is probably the \"per 1 m³\" denominator? Whatever — I'll preserve as columns.\n\nHmm wait, but for dual meters maybe the revolution counts relate to double volume. Doesn't matter for extraction.\n\nRows: The table uses rowspan for Volume. Rows:\n1. V=2.41: G=160, Qmax=250, Qmin=1, Qt=12.5, pmax=21, D=80 or 100, revolutions=414.462, per-m³=1, class=1.0 or 1.5\n2. V=2.41: G=250, Qmax=400, Qmin=2.5, Qt=20, pmax=21, D=80 or 100, rev=414.462, class 1.0 or 1.5\n3. V=3.96: G=250, Qmax=400, Qmin=2.5, Qt=20, pmax=21, D=100 or 150, rev=252.282, class 1.0/1.5\n4. V=3.96: G=400, Qmax=650, Qmin=4, Qt=32, pmax=21, D=100 or 150, rev=252.282, class 1.0/1.5\n5. V=3.96: G=650, Qmax=1000, Qmin=4, Qt=32, pmax=12, D=150 or 200, rev=252.282, class 1.0/1.5\n6. V=6.34: G=400, Qmax=650, Qmin=4, Qt=32, pmax=21, D=150 or 200, rev=157.676, class 1.0/1.5\n7. V=6.34: G=650, Qmax=1000, Qmin=6.5, Qt=50, pmax=21, D=150 or 200, rev=157.676, class 1.0/1.5\n\nNote G650 Qmax 1000: Qmin is 4 (row 5) vs 6.5 (row 7) — that's what the table says. Row 5: G=650, Qmax=1000, Qmin=4, Qt=32, pmax=12. Row 7: G=650, Qmax=1000, Qmin=6.5, Qt=50, pmax=21. OK.\n\nFootnote: \"*\" marker on Volume: \"On the name plate of the rotary meter the cyclic volume can be given in two possible formats: 1. with two digits behind the comma as stated in table 2 and 3, or 2. with a number containing 6 significant digits. In this case a HF pulse value can be accurately derived from the spinning rotors with an optical sensor.\"\n\nSecond table: meter size vs Minimum number of drums (before the comma / [second column unlabeled]) and control-element [m³].\nRows:\n- G6: 5, 3, 0.0002\n- G10 – G65: 6, 2, 0.002\n- G100 – G650: 7, 1, 0.02\n\nThe second drum column header is empty — likely \"after the comma\" (before/after comma digits). The header cell is empty, so I shouldn't fabricate \"after the comma\". Column names: \"drums_before_comma\" and unnamed. Hmm. Actually typical: minimum number of drums before the comma and after the comma. I'll name second \"drums_after_comma\"? That's fabrication of label but it's the obvious pairing. Rule: don't fabricate values; column naming requires some interpretation. I could name it \"drums_column_2_unlabeled\"... ugly. I think \"after_the_comma\" is reasonable inference; but safer: keep as \"\" → I'll use \"drums_after_the_comma\" with a note in description. Hmm. Let me name columns: [\"meter_size\", \"min_drums_before_comma\", \"min_drums_after_comma\", \"control_element\"]. And add description noting the second column header was empty in OCR. Actually the description field can mention that.\n\nAlso note the \"*\" footnote for volume. Include in footnotes[] with marker \"*\".\n\nModel family: This fragment covers FMR-Dual variants by G-value (160, 250, 400, 650) and volume (2.41, 3.96, 6.34 dm³). Since this is a fragment of one certificate, and part 1 presumably had type designations. The cert type: FMR-Dual rotary meter (R137 gas meters). certified_type category \"Gas meter\", type_designations [\"FMR-Dual\"]? The table header says \"Essential characteristics FMR-Dual\". I could include certified_type minimal. The instruction says \"for the content present in THIS fragment only. Omit sections that are not present.\" The table names FMR-Dual. I think including certified_type with type_designations [\"FMR-Dual\"] and category \"Gas meter\" is reasonable — category inference from R137? R137 is \"Gas meters\". The recommendation field: The number R137/2012-NL1-15.01 implies R137. Should I include recommendation? The fragment mentions R137 in the number. I think including recommendation with id \"R137\" edition... The number format \"R137/2012-NL1-15.01\" — 2012 could be edition? Actually for R137, the OIML R 137-1 (2012)? Hmm. R137/2012 — I'd say edition 2012? Edition as int: 2012. Hmm, risky. The example canonical: \"R60\" edition int. For R137/2012-NL1-15.01, the \"2012\" likely refers to the OIML R 137-1&2 edition year 2012? Actually OIML R 137-1 (2012) exists? R137 \"Gas meters\" 